# The Theorem (L_meta_951), on data. README items: Theorem, D1, D5, D8, L1, L5, T3, T4.
# Statement: a correct circuit with at most one NOT gate has gates(c) >= base(c) = sum over sets of (|S| - 1).
# Here: every witness circuit with n <= 9 (the circuits the project searched; incorrect ones are skipped).
corpus W = research/one-not/data/witnesses/*.json where n <= 9
claim forall c in W: correct(c) by lean            # hypothesis of the Theorem: the outputs compute the ORs
conjecture theorem: gates(c) >= base(c)            # the Theorem itself
conjecture count: slack(c) >= 0                    # D5/D8: |witness(empty cube)| >= base (the count behind it)
claim forall c in W: theorem(c)                    # the Theorem holds on every circuit of the corpus
claim forall c in W: count(c) by lean              # the count holds, one Lean theorem per circuit
count c in W: gates(c) = base(c)                   # tight cases: circuits with no spare gate
count c in W: slack(c) = 0                         # tight for the count
count c in W: nots(c) = 1                          # how many have the NOT gate

# One small circuit written here with ONE NOT gate that is read: x0 or x1 computed as x0 or (x1 and not x0).
# Sets {0,1} and {0,2,3}: base = 1 + 2 = 3, and 5 gates.
circuit w 4
  nx = not 0
  t = and 1 nx
  a = or 0 t
  b = or 0 2
  c = or b 3
  set 0 1 -> a
  set 0 2 3 -> c
end
show w
claim correct(w) by lean
claim nots(w) = 1
claim theorem(w)
claim gates(w) >= base(w)
claim forall cubes slack(w) >= 0 by lean           # D8: the count at every cube; with L5/L7 this is the whole Theorem for w
# the Fano formula: tight, 7 inputs
let fano = load research/one-not/data/witnesses/fano_formula.json
claim gates(fano) >= base(fano)
claim forall cubes slack(fano) >= 0 by lean
# WISH: claim forall c in W: nots(c) <= 1 -> gates(c) >= base(c)   (an implication, so the hypothesis of the Theorem is part of the claim)
# WISH: claim sets_ok(c)   (every set has >= 2 points, two sets share <= 1 point: the other hypotheses of L_meta_951)
